const TITLE = 'Few-shot calibration rather than architecture drives cross-participant EEG emotion recognition with full and six-electrode montages';
const AUTHORS = 'Saif Khan^1*^, Osama Bin Qashem^1^, K. M. Fahim A. Bari^1^, Rafid Rahman^1^ & Ahmed Wasif Reza^1^';
const AFFIL = '^1^Department of Computer Science and Engineering, East West University, Dhaka 1212, Bangladesh. *email: saifkhantdm@gmail.com';

const ABSTRACT_SR = 'Electroencephalography (EEG) emotion decoders must work for people whose data were never used in training, ideally on headsets with few electrodes, yet published cross-participant results differ in whether test-participant data are used. We benchmarked 26 models, including a two-stage contrastive model (CLISA-EWU), on four-class SEED-IV emotion recognition with repeated leave-one-participant-out validation (15 participants, three seeds) using 62 electrodes and a six-electrode frontal–temporal subset, scoring each held-out participant zero-shot and after calibration with 20 labelled windows (about 80 s). Zero-shot accuracy was modest (best 0.485, 95% CI 0.428–0.542; chance 0.25), and CLISA-EWU did not differ from a two-layer perceptron. Calibration added 16–21 percentage points to contrastive, adversarial and pseudo-label models but at most 2 points to supervised networks. Calibrated CLISA-EWU reached an accuracy of 0.678 (0.641–0.718; Cohen’s κ = 0.57, MCC = 0.58) with 62 electrodes and 0.620 (0.590–0.648; κ = 0.49) with six, retaining 91.4% of full-montage performance, and calibration made the model ranking consistent across participants (Kendall’s W 0.35 → 0.79). Brief user calibration, rather than architecture, explained most of the measurable gain.';

const ABSTRACT_IEEE = 'Cross-participant electroencephalography (EEG) emotion recognition is the key requirement for practical affective interfaces, yet reported accuracies are difficult to compare because studies differ in whether, and how, target-participant data are used. We present a controlled benchmark of 10 classical and 16 deep models, including CLISA-EWU, a two-stage contrastive pretraining and fine-tuning model, for four-class emotion recognition on SEED-IV. All models were evaluated with leave-one-participant-out cross-validation repeated over three seeds (45 runs per model and montage) using differential-entropy features from a 62-electrode montage and a six-electrode frontal–temporal subset (FP1, FP2, F7, F8, T7, T8). Each held-out participant was scored zero-shot and after few-shot calibration with 20 labelled windows. Zero-shot accuracy was modest for every model (best 0.485) and CLISA-EWU did not differ from a two-layer perceptron (p = 0.25). Calibration raised contrastive, adversarial and pseudo-label models by 16–21 percentage points but supervised networks by at most 2 points. After calibration, CLISA-EWU achieved the highest mean accuracy, 0.678 (95% CI 0.641–0.718) with 62 electrodes and 0.620 (0.590–0.648) with six electrodes, retaining 91.4% of full-montage performance; it significantly outperformed BYOL and PseudoLabel with 62 electrodes but was statistically indistinguishable from the next three models with six. We recommend that cross-participant EEG studies report zero-shot and calibrated performance separately, at the participant level.';

const KEYWORDS = ['EEG emotion recognition', 'cross-subject generalization', 'contrastive learning', 'few-shot calibration', 'wearable EEG', 'leave-one-subject-out validation'];

// ---- Text blocks ----
const INTRO = [
  'Emotional states shape attention, decision making and mental health, and objective markers of emotion could support adaptive human–computer interaction, neurofeedback and digital mental-health monitoring{russell,seediv}. Electroencephalography (EEG) is portable, inexpensive and has millisecond resolution, which makes it the most practical neural signal for everyday affect sensing{seed,deap}. Two obstacles separate laboratory results from use. First, a deployed decoder must work for a person whose data were never used in training, but EEG responses to the same stimulus differ markedly between individuals because of anatomy, electrode contact, baseline rhythms and appraisal{clisa,ganin,dan}. Second, research datasets are recorded with 32–64 electrodes, whereas wearable headsets carry far fewer.',
  'Many methods target the first obstacle, including domain-adversarial networks{ganin,bidann}, hemispheric-asymmetry models{bihdm}, graph neural networks{dgcnn,rgnn,sognn} and contrastive learning designed to make representations participant-invariant{clisa}. Reported cross-participant accuracies on SEED and SEED-IV range from about 53% to above 80%{dgcnn,dan,bidann,bihdm,rgnn,sognn,bfenet,attgraph,ssemerge} (Table {T:lit}). These values are rarely comparable. Studies differ in whether unlabelled EEG from the test participant is used during training (transductive adaptation), whether a few labelled test-participant samples are used to adapt the model (calibration), how windows are split, and whether the reported figure is a mean over participants or a best fold. Splitting matters because features from adjacent windows of the same trial are strongly correlated, so any overlap between fitting and testing data inflates accuracy{saeb,kapoor,liblock}.',
  'We therefore asked three questions under a single, fully reported protocol. How well do classical, supervised, self-supervised, adversarial and semi-supervised models decode four emotions in unseen participants without any target data? How much does brief labelled calibration change this picture, and does the effect depend on the training objective? How much performance is lost when a 62-electrode research montage is reduced to six frontal–temporal electrodes compatible with a headband-style wearable?',
];
const CONTRIB = [
  'A controlled benchmark of 26 models (10 classical, 16 deep) under repeated leave-one-participant-out validation, with 45 runs per model and montage and all per-fold outputs released.',
  'A separation of zero-shot and few-shot calibrated performance showing that the advantage of representation-learning objectives appears mainly after calibration, while zero-shot deep models do not outperform a two-layer perceptron.',
  'A participant-level quantification of electrode reduction: a six-electrode frontal–temporal montage retains 91.4% (95% CI 87.9–95.0%) of calibrated full-montage performance for CLISA-EWU.',
  'Participant-level statistics (bootstrap confidence intervals, paired Wilcoxon tests with Holm correction) together with class-level, representation and attribution analyses, and a software prototype for wearable deployment.',
];

const RELATED = [
  ['Domain adaptation and participant invariance', 'Early cross-participant work treated each participant as a domain. Deep adaptation networks aligned feature distributions with maximum mean discrepancy{dan}, and domain-adversarial training removed participant information through a gradient-reversal layer{ganin}. Bi-hemispheric adversarial and discrepancy models added neuroscientific priors on hemispheric asymmetry{bidann,bihdm}. Most of these methods are transductive: they use unlabelled EEG from the test participant during training, which is legitimate but must be distinguished from purely zero-shot evaluation.'],
  ['Graph and attention models', 'Graph neural networks model spatial relations between electrodes with learned or anatomical adjacency{dgcnn,rgnn,sognn}, and recent attention-based and frequency-band graph models report the highest SEED-IV accuracies{bfenet,attgraph,ssemerge}. Their reported values are summarised in Table {T:lit}, but differences in protocol, features and reporting unit preclude direct ranking.'],
  ['Contrastive and self-supervised learning', 'Contrastive objectives such as SimCLR{simclr} and bootstrap methods such as BYOL{byol} learn representations from augmented views without labels. CLISA{clisa} adapted contrastive learning to EEG by treating time-aligned responses of different participants to the same stimulus as positives, explicitly encouraging inter-participant alignment. Semi-supervised methods such as pseudo-labelling{pseudo} and MixMatch{mixmatch} exploit unlabelled data, including test-participant data when applied transductively.'],
  ['Reduced-electrode and wearable EEG', 'Frontal and temporal electrodes carry much of the emotion-related differential-entropy information{seed}, and knowledge distillation has been proposed to transfer information from dense to sparse montages{hinton}. However, few studies quantify how much cross-participant performance survives electrode reduction under the same model and protocol, with participant-level uncertainty.'],
];

