  'CLISA-EWU (Fig. {F:arch}) has an encoder of two fully connected layers (256 and 128 units, batch normalisation, ReLU, dropout 0.3), a two-layer projection head (64 units) and a linear classifier. In stage 1 (50 epochs), the encoder and projector were trained with the normalised temperature-scaled cross-entropy (NT-Xent) loss (temperature 0.5) on two views of each window: one with additive Gaussian noise (s.d. 0.1) and one with 30% of electrodes masked across all bands. In stage 2 (up to 50 epochs), the whole network was fine-tuned with cross-entropy (AdamW, learning rate 3 × 10^−4^, weight decay 10^−4^, cosine schedule, class-balanced sampling, early stopping with patience 15 on validation macro F1). Unlike the original CLISA{clisa}, which aligns time-locked responses of different participants to the same stimulus, the stage-1 objective contrasts augmented views of the same window and does not use participant or stimulus identity [[either implement cross-participant stimulus-aligned positives or rename the model to avoid implying explicit inter-subject alignment]]. Hyperparameters for all models are given in the project repository together with the code [[add them as a supplementary table]].',
