const DISCUSSION = [
  'In this benchmark of 26 models under one leave-one-participant-out protocol, three findings stand out. First, decoding four emotions in an unseen participant without any of their data was modest for every model, and the deep architectures, including the contrastive model of primary interest, did not clearly outperform a two-layer perceptron. Second, about 80 s of labelled calibration data raised accuracy by roughly 20 percentage points, but only for models trained with contrastive, domain-adversarial or pseudo-label objectives. Third, a six-electrode frontal–temporal montage retained about nine-tenths of calibrated full-montage performance.',
  'These findings change how cross-participant results should be read. Much of the published progress on SEED-IV has been attributed to architectural innovation, yet our results suggest that the protocol, and specifically whether and how target-participant data are used, may explain as much variance as the architecture. Under calibration, the ordering of model families inverted: supervised networks that matched self-supervised models zero-shot fell far behind them. Comparisons that mix zero-shot, transductive and calibrated results, or that compare a best fold with another study\'s mean, are therefore uninterpretable. We recommend that cross-participant EEG studies report zero-shot and calibrated results separately, specify the number and selection of calibration samples, keep calibration and test trials disjoint, and summarise results at the participant level with confidence intervals{tripod,kapoor}.',
  'The practical message for wearable affective computing is cautiously positive. A short calibration session is acceptable in most consumer and research workflows, and a headband carrying frontal and temporal electrodes appears to keep most of the decodable signal under calibration. That some self-supervised models lost nothing from electrode reduction suggests that the frontal–temporal subset contains much of the information these models exploit, consistent with the established role of frontal activity in affect{seed}. The attribution analysis indicates where the six-electrode montage falls short: fear relied on parieto-occipital electrodes outside the subset, and fear recall dropped most. A seven- or eight-electrode montage adding a parieto-occipital site is a natural next design to test. Nevertheless, the absolute calibrated accuracy (about 62% for four classes with six electrodes) and the large between-participant spread mean that individual predictions remain unreliable, and the system is not suitable for clinical decisions.',
  'This study has limitations. The cohort was small (15 participants), healthy and drawn from one laboratory, and emotions were elicited with film clips under controlled conditions; generalisation to other populations, naturalistic settings and clinical groups is unknown{seed,seedv,faced}. The six-electrode montage was simulated by selecting channels from a laboratory cap rather than recorded with a wearable device. Calibration windows were sampled across trials, so calibration and test windows could share a trial, which may have inflated calibrated accuracy{liblock}. Labels were assigned per film clip rather than per moment. We used precomputed features, so preprocessing choices could not be varied, and hyperparameters were not tuned separately for each model. Finally, the contrastive model contrasted augmented views of single windows rather than aligning participants explicitly, so its gains should not be attributed to inter-participant alignment.',
];
const CONCLUSION = 'In a controlled participant-independent benchmark, brief labelled calibration rather than architecture accounted for most of the measurable gain in EEG emotion recognition, and a six-electrode wearable-compatible montage retained about 91% of calibrated performance. Rigorous separation of zero-shot and calibrated evaluation, external validation and real wearable recordings are the next steps towards dependable EEG-based affect sensing.';

