const R_RANK = 'Friedman tests showed significant differences among the 15 deep models in every montage and protocol (Table {T:friedman}; all p < 0.001). Agreement between participants on the ordering of models was weak zero-shot (Kendall’s W = 0.35 for both montages) but strong after calibration (W = 0.79 and 0.77), so calibration made the ranking consistent across people (Fig. {F:cd}). After calibration, CLISA-EWU had the best average rank with 62 electrodes (1.40) and with six (2.67). The Nemenyi critical difference for 15 models and 15 participants was 5.54 ranks; by this conservative criterion, calibrated CLISA-EWU was distinguishable from every supervised network, MixMatch and the DANCE pair, but not from DANN (2.40), PseudoLabel (2.53), BYOL (4.07) or SimCLR (5.80) with 62 electrodes. Zero-shot, CLISA-EWU ranked first with 62 electrodes (4.53), but the deep and two-layer perceptrons were within 0.4 ranks of it, and with six electrodes it ranked third (5.93) behind BYOL (4.13) and SimCLR (4.87).';
const R_EFFECT = 'Paired effect sizes (Table {T:paired}) separate statistical from practical significance. After calibration with 62 electrodes, the advantage of CLISA-EWU over BYOL and SimCLR was very large (*d*~z~ = 2.04 and 2.62; *r*~rb~ = 1.00, with all 15 participants favouring CLISA-EWU), whereas its advantage over PseudoLabel and DANN was large in standardised terms (*d*~z~ = 0.98 and 0.82) but small in absolute terms (2.1 and 1.6 percentage points), and only the PseudoLabel difference survived Holm correction. With six electrodes, effects against the self-supervised and semi-supervised models were small (*d*~z~ = 0.14–0.30) and non-significant. Zero-shot, CLISA-EWU differed meaningfully only from EEG Conformer with six electrodes (5.3 points, *d*~z~ = 1.38, Holm p = 0.001); its differences from the perceptrons were negligible (*d*~z~ = 0.14–0.32).';
const R_MULTI = 'Table {T:cmext} reports the complete confusion-matrix metric set for CLISA-EWU and DANN. Calibrated CLISA-EWU with 62 electrodes reached an accuracy of 0.678, balanced accuracy 0.676, macro F1 0.673, Cohen’s κ 0.569 (95% CI 0.519–0.622) and MCC 0.577; with six electrodes the corresponding values were 0.620, 0.615, 0.611, 0.490 (0.449–0.530) and 0.499. On the Landis–Koch scale{landis}, calibrated agreement beyond chance was moderate (κ 0.41–0.60) for both montages, whereas zero-shot agreement was fair with 62 electrodes (κ = 0.31) and at the border of slight and fair with six (κ = 0.21). MCC tracked κ to within 0.01, macro and weighted F1 differed by at most 0.004, and the geometric mean of recalls (0.659 and 0.594 after calibration) stayed close to balanced accuracy, so the results were not driven by class imbalance or by neglect of any class. Mean specificity was high (0.893 and 0.873), indicating that errors were spread across classes rather than concentrated on one. Expressed as an information transfer rate, calibration raised throughput from 3.35 to 9.13 bits min^−1^ with 62 electrodes and from 1.51 to 6.85 bits min^−1^ with six. CLISA-EWU exceeded DANN on every metric in both montages and protocols, by 1.2–3.8 points of κ.';
const R_CLASS2 = 'Per-class metrics (Table {T:perclass}, Fig. {F:perclass}) and the mean confusion matrices over all 45 runs (Fig. {F:meancm}) show where the errors arose. After calibration with 62 electrodes, sad had the highest recall (0.733) but the lowest specificity (0.858), because it attracted errors from fear (16.9% of fear windows) and happy (14.3%); neutral had the highest precision (0.790) and F1 (0.728). Fear was the hardest class in both montages (F1 = 0.621 and 0.565) and was confused mostly with sad. Electrode reduction affected classes unequally: recall fell by 9.6 percentage points for happy, 7.4 for fear, 4.4 for sad and 2.9 for neutral. Zero-shot, the confusion matrices were diffuse and biased towards fear, which received 21.0% of neutral, 24.5% of sad and 21.3% of happy windows with 62 electrodes; calibration removed most of this bias. In the best single runs (Fig. {F:conf}), sad recall reached 100% with 62 electrodes.';
const R_VARDEC = 'A two-way decomposition (Table {T:vardec}) quantified the sources of this variability. Zero-shot with 62 electrodes, participant identity explained 96.9% of the variance in CLISA-EWU accuracy and the seed only 0.05% (ICC(3,1) = 0.95). After calibration, the participant share fell to 77.8%, residual variance rose to 19.3% and the ICC dropped to 0.70; with six electrodes the seed effect became significant (F = 4.49, p = 0.020; ICC = 0.53). Because the seed also determines which 20 windows are drawn for calibration, this increase reflects sensitivity to the choice of calibration samples, which argues for averaging over several calibration draws in practice.';
const R_CORR = 'Calibration gain was negatively correlated with zero-shot accuracy (Spearman ρ = −0.60, p = 0.017 with 62 electrodes; ρ = −0.58, p = 0.024 with six; Fig. {F:gain}a): participants who were decoded worst without calibration gained most, up to 39 percentage points for participant 11. Part of this association is expected from regression to the mean, because the gain contains the zero-shot score. Across montages, participants’ calibrated accuracies were strongly correlated (ρ = 0.80, p < 0.001) whereas their zero-shot accuracies were not (ρ = −0.05, p = 0.86; Fig. {F:gain}b), so after calibration a participant who is decoded well with 62 electrodes is also decoded well with six. Across models, participant rankings before and after calibration were almost identical for supervised networks (ρ = 0.94–1.00) but less so for the adaptable models (ρ = 0.43–0.93), consistent with calibration substantively changing only the latter.';
